WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:04.110
Film Courage: What's the most broke you've been in
your life and can you tell us a story

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:09.480
about it? R.L. Scott, Filmmaker: Hmmm?
In regards to filmmaking? Film Courage: Yes. Just

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:16.109
a time in your life? R.L.: Probably when I
made my first movie, my first feature

00:00:16.109 --> 00:00:25.099
film. I actually produced my first movie
for five hundred bucks

00:00:25.099 --> 00:00:33.570
shot it in three weeks between my house
my neighbor's house and a friend's house

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:41.360
that was like around the corner so so
between like a one-block radius like I

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:48.750
in the way the way it happened is sort
of like I this is actually interesting

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:54.539
so I had prep to do my first movie it
was it was a totally different idea than

00:00:54.539 --> 00:01:01.370
what I ended up going with I had done
pre-production for maybe two months and

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:08.640
it was all going to take place in like
this this really big building and the

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:13.890
day before we were gonna start shooting
the investor pulled out it's like Oh

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:19.680
something came up I can't you know I
can't do it so we'd prepped myself to

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:26.700
cast everybody we'd done all this prep
for two months we put like a deposit on

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:32.159
the location that we were going to use
the building we couldn't get the deposit

00:01:32.159 --> 00:01:40.049
back so I think that happened on a
Saturday we were supposed to start

00:01:40.049 --> 00:01:48.689
shooting Sunday night so I get the news
on Saturday I call up the cast between

00:01:48.689 --> 00:01:55.640
Saturday and Sunday I spoke to everybody
I said look this is what happened and

00:01:55.640 --> 00:02:02.280
yeah now we don't have a we don't have a
budget we don't have you know everything

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:07.170
we prepped for is pretty much we're at
square one now and I was like I know you

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:11.190
guys are ready to shoot they had already
took enough people to take enough days

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:15.610
and took off time to shoot
film I said look we're gonna shoot a

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:25.239
movie give me a day and I will give back
to you but but come and now we I guess

00:02:25.239 --> 00:02:29.200
we we couldn't shoot Sunday so it was
gonna be Monday I said just give me a

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.840
day
come Monday we're gonna start shooting

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:38.950
so the way I ended up shooting my first
feature was I would write the scenes

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:43.269
that we were gonna shoot that day in the
morning the cast would show up that day

00:02:43.269 --> 00:02:50.829
get the scenes we would go through the
scenes and basically we would shoot the

00:02:50.829 --> 00:02:55.359
scene so that the same day they got the
scenes we shot them there were action

00:02:55.359 --> 00:03:00.519
scenes in the film I would have to
choreograph the fights the day of and

00:03:00.519 --> 00:03:09.430
teach it to the actors the day we shot
them so we basically shot the film that

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:16.930
way every single day like I would shoot
that day and then do the work that night

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:23.819
to prep coming into the morning and we
so we shot the whole movie that way

00:03:23.889 --> 00:03:31.090
most of the actors had no martial arts
training but I actually developed now

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:36.310
this is also very interesting I've
developed a way of choreographing at

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:41.590
fight scenes over the years and making
actors look like they know how to fight

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:47.549
you know and I use the sword as it's
like this chain chain length method

00:03:47.549 --> 00:03:54.010
where you take three or four techniques
we call it an exchange right so one

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:58.780
exchange would be through four
techniques you teach those three or four

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:04.690
techniques to the actors it's sort of a
short a short burst of energy versus

00:04:04.690 --> 00:04:09.370
like just shooting a fight full-on and
people getting tired and then that's how

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:14.169
accidents happened right but you could
get a short burst of energy out of them

00:04:14.169 --> 00:04:20.949
for three or four techniques and and
then the Edit you use the last technique

00:04:20.949 --> 00:04:27.500
of that exchange you would use that to
cut into

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:33.330
the first technique of the next exchange
and I actually learned that method from

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:37.410
looking at like when I was young you
know the barrel of monkeys where they

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:41.220
would have the hands that would connect
and you pull them out of the theatre of

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:49.560
the barrel of monkeys so that's how I
learned to to choreograph and then cut

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:54.720
the way I would cut is I would use the
last part of datak stage as the first

00:04:54.720 --> 00:05:01.470
move so even though you're shooting
these tiny little bursts of action when

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:07.020
you cut them together they seem seamless
because you always use the material from

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:13.710
the last punch
so I guess to simplify that if if I have

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:16.740
an exchange and the last the last
technique you've met exchanges a

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:22.350
straight punch right the next technique
in the new exchange is that same

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:28.910
straight punch you know that me so that
you're able to carry that over and that

00:05:28.910 --> 00:05:35.610
I'm using the straight punch from the
second exchange not the last technique

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:40.380
of that exchange so that way it just
seems like a constant stream of energy

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:47.669
like this non-stop sort of action
sequence even though it was like these

00:05:47.669 --> 00:05:53.850
really tiny bursts so that way you can
keep everybody safe and you can sort of

00:05:53.850 --> 00:06:05.400
control how you know how things are done
and then you you you you use composition

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:09.630
based on like what's going to make that
technique look the best so you're able

00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:15.780
to hide their flaws and and sort of show
their strengths hi their weaknesses that

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:23.820
sort of thing but um but yeah I think
that whole three weeks of making my

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:30.270
first feature with I had no script I had
no you know I had to write it as we shot

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:38.460
and they had no prep we had no money you
know that's probably like the brokest

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:43.650
I've been but that but that movie got
like worldwide distribution and it's

00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:51.840
sort of yeah that also showed me like
how much power you really have like as a

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:58.069
content creator like how you can sort of
create something and even if it's in a

00:06:58.069 --> 00:07:04.319
rough situation it can it can still do
great things and it can still be

00:07:04.319 --> 00:07:11.569
received well and yeah that just opened
up a world of possibilities after that

00:07:11.569 --> 00:07:17.039
and then how confident did that make you
going forward before every other shoot

00:07:17.039 --> 00:07:21.569
because you basically had the bottom
fall out yeah and you still made it work

00:07:21.569 --> 00:07:24.389
somehow I mean you had people that it
could have been very mad it took all

00:07:24.389 --> 00:07:28.229
this time off whatever everybody was
probably like heartbroken you made it

00:07:28.229 --> 00:07:31.800
work somehow
how does that build your confidence yeah

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:38.130
I mean it it was great because I'm I
never I never gave them the impression

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:44.159
that things were just you know what I
mean I was very calm you know this

00:07:44.159 --> 00:07:51.479
happened but this is what we're gonna do
you know like I never I never made it

00:07:51.479 --> 00:08:00.469
feel like it was a problem like just
this thing that we couldn't get past or

00:08:00.469 --> 00:08:05.550
you know I just I don't know is this the
way I delivered it to him I guess and

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:11.099
then I think also because they had seen
a lot of the other work I'd done like

00:08:11.099 --> 00:08:14.280
the shorts and stuff like that
I think they were pretty confident that

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:21.180
like you know we'd be okay but I was
very much I was never like oh my god

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:24.810
they did this and I don't know what
we're gonna you know I never never did

00:08:24.810 --> 00:08:29.729
that I was just like hey you know we're
gonna just change gears a little bit you

00:08:29.729 --> 00:08:38.010
know this is what happened we're still
making a film yeah so I think just being

00:08:38.010 --> 00:08:47.339
calm and never sort of letting on how
crazy things have gotten I think I think

00:08:47.339 --> 00:08:54.040
that helped a lot and to have it go so
wrong and to be able to come

00:08:54.040 --> 00:09:01.480
the other side was something that was
like tangible like yeah that changed

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:07.930
everything for me like you know I just
didn't feel like it was that I didn't

00:09:07.930 --> 00:09:11.860
feel like it was anything I couldn't do
at that point I just felt like you just

00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:16.389
find a way like you stay calm you figure
it out

00:09:16.389 --> 00:09:24.459
you problem-solve and because if you
strip away all the essentials right when

00:09:24.459 --> 00:09:32.440
you get down to what the film really is
its location its actors you know

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:40.180
